    pr_ref:  # bf76
    description: "MEASURED ON A LIVE SELF-SURVEY (dev 8f416a87c3, 154,505 edges, 39,297\nproduction-scoped). 80 of the 81 opaque launch sites are ALSO counted as\nuncatalogued modules, so the disclosure and the blocker are the same edge.\n\nTHE POSITIVE CONTROL, three arms, each on a ONE-EDGE substrate with the\nsupported-language set matched to that substrate (passing every language lets\nthe analyzer-blind check fire first and drive qualifying_only False for an\nunrelated reason - that confound was hit and corrected mid-measurement):\n\n  A  one bash command launch (bash:id:0-0:id:external_symbol), alone\n       unknown = ['id']          opaque = ['id.id']     qualifying_only=False\n  B  one python subprocess.run call, alone\n       unknown = []              opaque = ['subprocess.run']  qualifying_only=True\n  C  CONTROL, catalogued builtins.open, alone\n       unknown = []              opaque = []            complete=True\n\nA is the defect: ONE edge lands in BOTH sets and cancels itself. B shows the\npython path is immune - not by design, but because `subprocess.run` happens to\ncarry a catalogue row, so `classify_call` matches and step (1) of\n`_uncatalogued_external_modules` skips it. C shows the gate still works for an\nordinary catalogued call.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, and it is stronger than a coverage gap: `qualifying_only` is\ncomputed as `not unknown`, and a producer-stamped launch adds its own module to\n`unknown`. So for any repo containing a shell script, the fourth verdict\n(confirmed_with_caveats / CAVEAT_OPAQUE_BOUNDARY, ADR-0016 section 4) is\nunreachable BY CONSTRUCTION: the qualification can only be granted when no\nlaunch exists, and when no launch exists there is nothing to qualify. The\nverdict added on 2026-08-13 for exactly this consumer cannot fire on the\nsubstrate it was built for. NO AMOUNT OF CATALOGUE WORK CHANGES THIS.\n\nROOT CAUSE, and it is a shape this repo has paid for before (INV-larol, and\nLIVE.md's \"ONE FACT, TWO HOMES\"): there are TWO channels that know a call is a\nlaunch - the catalogue (`declares_opaque_crossing`) and the PRODUCER STAMP\n(`meta.io_boundary in PRODUCER_OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES`, which the bash analyzer sets\nbecause ADR-0016 rules out a bash io_primitives catalogue).\n`_opaque_launch_sites` was taught to check the producer stamp FIRST, precisely\nso a launch keeps its opacity no matter what a catalogue says.\n`_uncatalogued_external_modules` was never taught the same thing: it asks\n`classify_call` only. So the two functions give opposite answers about one\nedge - one says \"this is a launch I have disclosed\", the other says \"this is a\nmodule I hold no rows for\".\n\nTHE FIX IS THE SKIP, NOT A NEW PREDICATE. An opaque launch is an EXAMINED call:\nwe know exactly what it is and where it goes, and its opacity is already\nreported through the caveat channel built for it. Counting it a second time\nthrough the blindness channel is the category error - \"I could not look\" versus\n\"I looked, control left the process here, and I have named the site\". Both\nconsumers must ask the producer channel through ONE predicate rather than two\ncopies, or this drifts again.\n\nDIRECTION, stated because it is the expensive one: this moves verdicts TOWARD\nconfirming. It does not weaken disclosure - every site it stops counting as\nunknown is still NAMED in the caveat, so the reader loses nothing and gains the\ndistinction between a named opaque door and an unexamined one. Measured effect\non the self-proof: 212 uncatalogued modules -> 134 (78 bash command launches\ndrop). That alone does NOT flip any verdict - the remaining 134 still block -\nso it must not be mistaken for a fix for INV-dabuf.\n\nNOT SWEPT: `env` appears both as a bash `env` COMMAND launch and as the\nsynthetic `env` module INV-jurif introduced for environment reads\n(bash:env:0-0:env.environ:attribute). They share a module slot. Whether that\ncollides is unmeasured."  # bf76
    fields:  # bf76
      statement: "A call disclosed as an opaque launch must not ALSO be counted as an uncatalogued module: the two are different findings ('I named this door' vs 'I could not look'), and counting one edge as both makes ADR-0016 section 4's confirmed_with_caveats verdict unreachable for every repo that contains a launch. VIOLATED: 80 of 81 opaque launch sites on hypergumbo's own self-survey are also in the uncatalogued-module set; a one-edge positive control (bash 'id') returns unknown=['id'] and opaque=['id.id'] simultaneously, qualifying_only=False."  # bf76
      root_cause: "Two channels know a call is a launch - the catalogue (declares_opaque_crossing) and the producer stamp (meta.io_boundary in PRODUCER_OPAQUE_BOUNDARIES, set by the bash analyzer because ADR-0016 rules out a bash io_primitives catalogue). _opaque_launch_sites consults the producer stamp FIRST by design (INV-larol); _uncatalogued_external_modules consults classify_call ONLY and never asks the producer channel, so it re-counts a disclosed launch as blindness. The python path is immune only incidentally, because subprocess.run carries a catalogue row that classify_call matches."  # bf76
